  "title": "linked lists, pointer rewiring, reversal, and cycles",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "A linked list stores values in separate nodes connected by references instead of contiguous indexed storage.",
    "Singly linked nodes store a next reference; doubly linked nodes also store a previous reference, trading space for reverse navigation.",
    "Inserting or deleting a known node can change a constant number of links, but finding the location still requires traversal.",
    "A dummy head node simplifies operations that may change the actual head, including deletion and merging.",
    "Pointer rewiring requires saving the next reference before overwriting a link and maintaining a clear invariant at every step.",
    "Fast and slow pointers can locate a middle node, detect a cycle, or express a fixed distance between two positions.",
    "Floyd cycle detection uses a fast pointer moving two links and a slow pointer moving one; a meeting proves a cycle exists.",
    "Reversal changes link direction in place using previous, current, and next references, with empty and one-node cases handled explicitly.",
    "Node identity and node value are different: intersection means sharing the same node object, not merely equal values.",
    "Linked lists provide sequential access and pointer flexibility but usually have poorer locality and no constant-time indexing."
  ],
